WEBVTT

00:00:15.308 --> 00:00:18.038
<v><b>Geoffrey:</b></v> Something that amazes tourists when they travel

00:00:18.063 --> 00:00:23.326
to Switzerland is the amount of multilingual information they are provided with all the time.

00:00:23.796 --> 00:00:26.005
So, for example, if you are traveling on the train,

00:00:26.005 --> 00:00:30.198
you will hear announcements in three different languages consecutively.

00:00:30.613 --> 00:00:35.330
For me this is really mundane, but I suppose if you are not used to it, it might be surprising.

00:00:36.037 --> 00:00:37.454
And when I came to London,

00:00:37.454 --> 00:00:43.013
I was absolutely shocked to see how many people were jaywalking all the time, everywhere.

00:00:43.282 --> 00:00:48.575
I did this once in my home country on a single carriageway in a, in a little town.

00:00:48.924 --> 00:00:53.397
I crossed the street with a red pedestrian traffic light and I got fined.

00:00:53.969 --> 00:00:56.727
So I decided I would never do that again.

00:01:06.832 --> 00:01:10.734
<v><b>Beata:</b></v> In Poland, it’s completely normal to wear slippers at home.

00:01:10.734 --> 00:01:14.816
And also our guests are offered a pair of slippers.

00:01:15.356 --> 00:01:19.323
And I can understand, of course, that it might be surprising for foreigners.

00:01:19.767 --> 00:01:25.702
What surprised me abroad, for example, when I was, um, in Italy for the first time,

00:01:26.099 --> 00:01:30.206
I was a bit surprised that people eat dinner so late.

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:38.509
Because in Poland we dinner at about six and at six, the restaurants were still er empty.

00:01:48.257 --> 00:01:54.748
<v><b>Jorge:</b></v> Something that often surprises visitors to Brazil is how informal and open people can be.

00:01:55.486 --> 00:02:01.015
For example, it’s common for strangers to start conversations in lines or on public transportation.

00:02:01.722 --> 00:02:05.306
And also, our sense of personal space is quite different.

00:02:06.219 --> 00:02:10.217
People here do stand close to each other when talking,

00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:12.634
and we often use a lot of physical contact,

00:02:12.633 --> 00:02:16.929
like touching someone’s arm in order to show friendliness.

00:02:19.307 --> 00:02:21.222
The first time I travelled abroad,

00:02:21.261 --> 00:02:28.752
I went to Germany and I was surprised by how punctual and organized everything was.

00:02:29.109 --> 00:02:35.568
Buses and trains arrived exactly on time and people seemed to plan their days really carefully.

00:02:36.398 --> 00:02:43.578
In Brazil, things tend to be a bit more relaxed and it’s not uncommon for schedules to be more flexible.

00:02:43.959 --> 00:02:47.415
I had to get used to double-checking timetables,

00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:51.490
but I also appreciated how efficient everything was.